Australian context. These are legal constraints, not preferences.

TGA: botulinum toxin and dermal fillers are Schedule 4 prescription medicines.
Referring to them in advertising directed at the public is prohibited, including
brand names, category terms such as "anti wrinkle injections", nicknames, and
any price or discount attached to them. The practitioner exemption covers a
clinician advising their own patient, not an automated SMS sent by an agency.
Enforced via a per-clinic blocked terms list checked against both model output
AND knowledge base content.

Privacy Act APP 8: enquiry text and mobile numbers stay in Sydney. Notification
emails and SMS therefore contain clinic name and a dashboard link only. No
question text, no mobile number, no name. Resend stores account data in the US
regardless of send region, which is why the payload must be empty of PII.

Spam Act: inbound enquiry implies consent to reply. Consent records are stored
per contact per clinic. Opt-out is tracked per contact per clinic and checked
before every send.

SMS formatting, from the client's existing skill files:
- No emojis, no em dashes, no smart quotes or ellipsis characters
- Plain casual Australian tone, no email-style signoffs
- Match the customer's message length, do not out-text them
- One question per message
- Segments cost money. Cap draft length and show a live segment count.
